# Access Control – Secure AI Data Pipeline

## Purpose
This document defines how access to data and encryption keys is controlled for both human users and non-human identities (services).

## Core Principles
- Least privilege
- Separation of duties (especially between data access and key administration)
- Identity-based access (no shared credentials)
- Clear distinction between human and non-human identities
- Auditability of access

## Identity Types

| Identity Type          | Purpose                              | Example                     |
|------------------------|--------------------------------------|-----------------------------|
| Human users            | Administration, review, support      | Security engineers, data owners |
| Ingestion service      | Write new documents                  | Ingestion service account   |
| AI / RAG service       | Read and decrypt allowed data        | RAG service account         |
| Key administrators     | Manage KMS keys                      | Limited security team role  |

## Access Control Model

### Knowledge Base Access
- Default deny.
- Write access limited to the ingestion service identity.
- Read / decrypt access limited to the AI/RAG service identity (and approved human roles if needed).
- Access can be further restricted by data classification when supported.

### Key Access (KMS)
- Only specific service identities are allowed to use (encrypt/decrypt) the CMEK.
- Key administration rights are held by a very small set of roles and are separate from day-to-day data access.
- Key usage is logged.

### Human Access
- Human users do not receive direct database credentials or broad read access by default.
- Administrative access is granted temporarily and audited when required.

## Example High-Level Roles

| Role                        | Can Write Data | Can Read/Decrypt Data | Can Manage Keys |
|----------------------------|----------------|-----------------------|-----------------|
| Ingestion Service          | Yes            | No                    | No              |
| AI / RAG Service           | No             | Yes (limited)         | Use only        |
| Security Administrator     | No             | Limited / Break-glass | Yes             |
| Standard Human User        | No             | No                    | No              |

## Policy Enforcement Points
- IAM policies on the knowledge base
- IAM policies on the KMS keys
- Service identity configuration (Workload Identity / service accounts)
- Application-level checks (where classification is enforced)

## Logging & Review
- All access to sensitive data and key usage is logged with identity context.
- Access policies should be reviewed regularly.
- Break-glass / emergency access procedures should be defined and monitored.

## Residual Risks
- Over-permissioned service accounts
- Standing privileges that are never reviewed
- Insider threats with legitimate elevated roles
- Misconfiguration of IAM policies
